<p>{% person "harry-l-hartman" %}Harry L. Hartman{% endperson %} purchased Mandan's two principal entertainment venues—the Grand Theatre and Rex Theater—from {% person "rex-sanderson" %}Rex Sanderson{% endperson %} in early 1915. Hartman closed the Grand in October 1916 and continued operating the Rex while construction delayed the opening of his new show house.</p>

<p>The Hartman Photoplay Company initially planned to open the remodeled, 650-seat Hartman House on September 1, 1916. Construction pushed the opening of the renamed Palace Theatre to November 17. At a time when structural fires were common, its {% inflation 25000, 1916 %} brick-and-concrete construction was promoted as thoroughly fireproof.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/palace-theatre/401-1910-palace-theater.jpg" alt="The Palace Theatre in Mandan">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Seven hundred people attended opening night. A newspaper reported that although the six vaudeville acts and motion pictures pleased the crowd, the theater itself was the principal attraction. Its comfortable leather-bottomed seats provided clear views of the stage. Black wall strips formed panel and latticework designs accented by flower boxes and candle lamps.</p>

<p>The building was described as first-class from the box office to the dressing rooms beneath the stage. Mandan Electric Company installed more than 650 light bulbs in the canopy, exterior sign, lobby, auditorium, and stage fixtures.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/palace-theatre/444-1916-ad-mandan-pioneer.jpg" alt="1916 advertisement for the Palace Theatre">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The Palace opened with <em>The Pawn of Fate</em>, starring {% person "george-beban" %}George Beban{% endperson %} as Pierre, a French peasant who aspired to become a great painter and traveled to Paris. The <em>Mandan Pioneer</em> praised Beban's ability to move audiences from laughter to tears and back again.</p>

